\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Reed-Bed Encroachment and Wader Decline\ in a Managed Wetland}
\author{L. Okafor and P. Reyes}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Managed wetlands trade one succession pressure for another:
holding water levels steady favours reed-bed expansion, and
the closed sward that follows is poor habitat for wading
birds. The survey protocol we follow throughout is the
standard vegetation transect method of \cite{surveyguide2023},
which fixes quadrat spacing and growth-stage scoring so that
seasons can be compared directly.

\section{Survey design}
Twelve transects were laid perpendicular to the main drain
and walked every fortnight from March to October. Reed
fraction was scored in situ, and wader counts from the hide
were averaged over the same windows. The hydrological
context follows the long-term series of \cite{marsh2017}.

\section{Results}
Reed cover rose from 41 to 58 percent over the study window,
and wader counts fell by roughly a third over the same
period. The decline was steepest on the northern transects,
where encroachment closed the last open pools in 2024.

\section{Discussion}
The coupling between vegetation closure and wader decline
matches the mechanism proposed by \cite{delacroix2021} for
grazed marshes: birds abandon sites not when food disappears
but when sightlines close. Rotational cutting on the northern
bank is the obvious first remedy.

